/* Unit 1: How We Study the Mind. Notes data and key terms for the revision site. */
window.UNIT={"id":"unit1","eyebrow":"Unit 1","title":"How We Study the Mind","sections":{"A":"History and Perspectives","B":"Psychology as a Science","C":"Cognitive Biases","D":"Research Methods","E":"Correlation and Statistics","F":"Research Ethics"},"terms":{"A":[{"term":"Psychology","def":"The scientific study of behavior and mental processes. Psychology is an empirical science, not just therapy or common sense."},{"term":"Structuralism","def":"An early school led by Wundt and Titchener. Goal: break conscious experience into its basic elements using introspection. Key limitation: subjective and hard to replicate."},{"term":"Introspection","def":"The method used by structuralists, trained self-reporting of one's own immediate conscious experience. Limitation: results are subjective and cannot be independently verified."},{"term":"Functionalism","def":"An early school led by William James, influenced by Darwin. Asked how mental processes help people adapt and survive, rather than what they are made of."},{"term":"Psychoanalysis","def":"Freud's framework proposing that behavior is driven by unconscious motives, unresolved childhood conflicts, and repressed desires."},{"term":"Behaviorism","def":"School founded by Watson and Skinner. Only observable, measurable behavior should be studied. All behavior is learned through conditioning."},{"term":"Gestalt Psychology","def":"Argued that perception is holistic: the whole is greater than the sum of its parts. We naturally see organized wholes, not isolated elements."},{"term":"Humanistic Psychology","def":"Led by Maslow and Rogers. Emphasizes free will, human potential, and self-actualization. Rejected the determinism of behaviorism and psychoanalysis."},{"term":"Cognitive Perspective","def":"Focuses on internal mental processes: how people think, remember, interpret, and solve problems. Behavior is shaped by how we process information."},{"term":"Biological Perspective","def":"Explains behavior through brain structure, neural chemistry, genetics, and physiology. Also called the neuroscience perspective."},{"term":"Evolutionary Perspective","def":"Applies Darwin's natural selection to psychological traits. Asks how a behavior helped our ancestors survive and reproduce."},{"term":"Social-Cultural Perspective","def":"Explains behavior through the influence of culture, social norms, and group context. What is considered normal varies across societies."},{"term":"Biopsychosocial Model","def":"An integrative framework proposing that behavior is best understood by examining biological, psychological, and social-cultural factors together, not in isolation."},{"term":"Self-Actualization","def":"The humanistic concept of reaching one's full potential. The highest level in Maslow's hierarchy of needs, achieved after lower-order needs are met."},{"term":"Empiricism","def":"The principle that knowledge comes from observation and experience, not from authority or intuition alone. The philosophical foundation of psychological science."}],"B":[{"term":"Scientific Method","def":"A cycle of inquiry: observe and question, form a hypothesis, test it, analyze results, draw conclusions, then replicate and report."},{"term":"Hypothesis","def":"A specific, testable prediction about the relationship between two or more variables. A good hypothesis is falsifiable."},{"term":"Theory","def":"A well-supported explanatory framework that organizes observations and generates testable predictions. Not a guess: a theory is built on accumulated evidence."},{"term":"Falsifiability","def":"The requirement that a hypothesis can in principle be proven wrong by evidence. If no evidence could ever disprove a claim, it is unscientific."},{"term":"Operational Definition","def":"A precise, measurable specification of how a variable will be defined and measured in a specific study. Enables replication and consistency."},{"term":"Replication","def":"Repeating a study to test whether its findings can be reproduced across different participants or contexts. Essential for scientific confidence."},{"term":"Peer Review","def":"Evaluation of research by qualified experts before publication. The primary quality-control mechanism in science, but does not guarantee correctness."},{"term":"Scientific Attitude","def":"Three qualities working together: curiosity (openness to investigating questions), skepticism (demanding evidence), and humility (willingness to revise conclusions)."},{"term":"Curiosity","def":"The scientific quality of genuine openness to asking questions and following evidence wherever it leads, even when findings are surprising."},{"term":"Skepticism","def":"The scientific quality of demanding good evidence before accepting a claim, including claims made by authorities or popular belief."},{"term":"Humility","def":"The scientific quality of being willing to revise or abandon conclusions when new evidence demands it. The opposite of overconfidence."}],"C":[{"term":"Cognitive Bias","def":"A predictable, systematic error in thinking that affects everyone, including researchers. Understanding biases is a practical tool for improving reasoning."},{"term":"Hindsight Bias","def":"The tendency to believe, after learning an outcome, that one could have predicted it all along. Also called the 'I knew it all along' phenomenon."},{"term":"Confirmation Bias","def":"The tendency to seek, notice, and remember information that confirms pre-existing beliefs, while filtering out contradicting evidence."},{"term":"Overconfidence Bias","def":"Holding more certainty in one's own judgments than the evidence actually warrants. A well-documented error even among intelligent, careful thinkers."},{"term":"Planning Fallacy","def":"The systematic tendency to underestimate how long tasks will take, how much they will cost, and how much effort they will require. A specific form of overconfidence bias."},{"term":"Illusory Correlation","def":"Perceiving a relationship between two variables that does not actually exist, or is much weaker than perceived. Often driven by memorable pairings."},{"term":"Self-Sealing Claim","def":"A claim structured so that no evidence could count against it, because disconfirming results are explained away. Such claims are unfalsifiable and therefore unscientific."}],"D":[{"term":"Experiment","def":"A research method that manipulates at least one variable to observe its effect on another. The only method that allows causal conclusions."},{"term":"Independent Variable (IV)","def":"The variable deliberately manipulated by the researcher. The presumed cause. What is changed between conditions."},{"term":"Dependent Variable (DV)","def":"The variable measured to detect the effect of the IV. The presumed outcome or effect."},{"term":"Confounding Variable","def":"An uncontrolled variable that varies alongside the IV and could provide an alternative explanation for results."},{"term":"Random Assignment","def":"Placing participants into conditions entirely by chance. Distributes pre-existing individual differences across groups and enables causal conclusions."},{"term":"Control Group","def":"The group that does not receive the treatment. Provides the baseline for comparison with the experimental group."},{"term":"Experimental Group","def":"The group that receives the treatment or active version of the IV."},{"term":"Single-Blind Procedure","def":"Participants do not know which condition they are in. Reduces participant expectation effects."},{"term":"Double-Blind Procedure","def":"Neither participants nor the researchers interacting with them know the group assignments. Controls for both participant and experimenter bias."},{"term":"Placebo Effect","def":"Improvement resulting from a participant's expectation of treatment, not from the treatment itself. Controlled by blinding procedures."},{"term":"Naturalistic Observation","def":"Observing behavior in a real setting without manipulation or intervention. High ecological validity but cannot establish causation."},{"term":"Case Study","def":"An in-depth investigation of a single individual or small group. Rich in detail but findings may not generalize to others."},{"term":"Correlational Study","def":"Measures two or more variables to identify relationships without manipulation. Cannot establish causation."},{"term":"Meta-Analysis","def":"Combines and statistically analyzes results across many existing studies. More reliable than a single study but limited by the quality of included studies."},{"term":"Random Selection","def":"Drawing participants from a population so every member has an equal chance of inclusion. Supports generalizability of findings. Different from random assignment."}],"E":[{"term":"Correlation Coefficient (r)","def":"A number from -1.0 to +1.0 indicating the direction and strength of a relationship between two variables."},{"term":"Positive Correlation","def":"As one variable increases, so does the other. r is positive. Example: hours studying and exam scores tend to rise together."},{"term":"Negative Correlation","def":"As one variable increases, the other decreases. r is negative. Example: more sleep, fewer errors."},{"term":"Strength of Correlation","def":"How close r is to 1.0 or -1.0. Roughly: weak = 0.00-0.39, moderate = 0.40-0.69, strong = 0.70-1.00. The sign shows direction, not strength."},{"term":"Directionality Problem","def":"When two variables are correlated, we cannot tell which one causes the other. Both causal directions are possible."},{"term":"Third Variable Problem","def":"A correlation between two variables may be caused by a third unmeasured variable that independently influences both."},{"term":"Mean","def":"The arithmetic average of all scores. Sensitive to extreme outliers. Can be misleading in skewed distributions."},{"term":"Median","def":"The middle value when scores are ordered. Not affected by extreme scores. Better measure of center for skewed data."},{"term":"Mode","def":"The most frequently occurring value. A dataset can have more than one mode or no mode at all."},{"term":"Standard Deviation (SD)","def":"The average distance of scores from the mean. Small SD means scores cluster tightly; large SD means scores are spread widely."},{"term":"Normal Distribution","def":"A symmetrical bell-shaped curve where most scores cluster around the mean. Mean, median, and mode are equal in a perfectly normal distribution."},{"term":"Positive Skew","def":"Tail extends to the right. Most scores cluster at the low end with a few unusually high scores pulling the mean above the median."},{"term":"Negative Skew","def":"Tail extends to the left. Most scores cluster at the high end with a few low outliers pulling the mean below the median."},{"term":"Percentile Rank","def":"The proportion of scores at or below a given score. The 80th percentile means performing better than 80% of test-takers. Not the same as percentage correct."},{"term":"Regression Toward the Mean","def":"Extreme scores tend to move closer to the average on re-measurement, regardless of any external cause. A statistical tendency, not a psychological one."}],"F":[{"term":"Informed Consent","def":"Participants must be fully informed about the study and voluntarily agree to take part before it begins. Must include the right to withdraw."},{"term":"Informed Assent","def":"When participants are under 18, their personal agreement is required in addition to parental or guardian consent. Parental consent alone is not sufficient."},{"term":"Confidentiality","def":"Participant data must be stored and reported so individuals cannot be identified. Removing a name alone does not guarantee anonymity."},{"term":"Protection from Harm","def":"Researchers must minimize physical and psychological risks to participants. Risk must be proportionate to the potential benefit of the research."},{"term":"Deception","def":"Misleading participants about the true purpose of a study. Permitted only when necessary, risk is minimal, and debriefing follows."},{"term":"Debriefing","def":"Full explanation of the study's true purpose provided to participants after data collection. Mandatory when deception was used. Participants may withdraw data."},{"term":"IRB (Institutional Review Board)","def":"An independent ethics committee that reviews research proposals before data collection begins. Ensures risks are minimized and participants are protected."},{"term":"Anonymity","def":"A stronger protection than confidentiality: no identifying information is collected at all. Participants cannot be linked to their responses."}]},"related":[["Biopsychosocial Model","Biological Perspective"],["Biopsychosocial Model","Social-Cultural Perspective"],["Biopsychosocial Model","Cognitive Perspective"],["Structuralism","Introspection"],["Humanistic Psychology","Self-Actualization"],["Scientific Attitude","Curiosity"],["Scientific Attitude","Skepticism"],["Scientific Attitude","Humility"],["Hypothesis","Falsifiability"],["Falsifiability","Self-Sealing Claim"],["Cognitive Bias","Hindsight Bias"],["Cognitive Bias","Confirmation Bias"],["Cognitive Bias","Overconfidence Bias"],["Cognitive Bias","Planning Fallacy"],["Cognitive Bias","Illusory Correlation"],["Overconfidence Bias","Planning Fallacy"],["Experiment","Independent Variable (IV)"],["Control Group","Experimental Group"],["Single-Blind Procedure","Double-Blind Procedure"],["Double-Blind Procedure","Placebo Effect"],["Correlation Coefficient (r)","Strength of Correlation"],["Correlation Coefficient (r)","Positive Correlation"],["Correlation Coefficient (r)","Negative Correlation"],["Confidentiality","Anonymity"],["Informed Consent","Informed Assent"],["Deception","Debriefing"]],"mistakes":{"A":[{"think":"cognitive and psychodynamic are the same because both are about the mind.","actually":"cognitive is about conscious thought processes. Psychodynamic is about unconscious drives and early experience."},{"think":"the biopsychosocial model is just another perspective.","actually":"it is a framework that brings several perspectives together, not a single-level explanation."},{"think":"behaviorism and the social-cultural perspective are the same because both involve the environment.","actually":"behaviorism explains behavior through a person's own history of reinforcement. The social-cultural perspective explains it through cultural norms and group influence."},{"think":"humanistic and cognitive are similar because both rejected behaviorism.","actually":"humanistic psychology is about free will, growth, and personal experience. Cognitive psychology is about how information is processed and interpreted."}],"B":[{"think":"if a study was peer reviewed, its findings must be correct.","actually":"peer review catches obvious errors but does not guarantee correctness. Findings also need to be replicated."},{"think":"a falsifiable hypothesis is one that has been proven false.","actually":"falsifiable means it could in principle be proven false. Whether it actually is false is a separate question."},{"think":"an operational definition is the same as a theory.","actually":"a theory is a broad explanatory framework. An operational definition is the specific way one variable is measured in one study."},{"think":"one successful replication confirms a finding.","actually":"one replication helps, but confidence builds through consistent replication across different samples and settings."}],"C":[{"think":"hindsight bias and confirmation bias are the same because both distort thinking about the past.","actually":"hindsight bias is feeling you knew the outcome all along. Confirmation bias is selectively processing evidence to support a belief you already hold."},{"think":"illusory correlation means a correlation that has been proven false.","actually":"it means perceiving a pattern that does not exist, or is much weaker than it seems. The key word is perceiving."},{"think":"overconfidence only means being arrogant.","actually":"it is a specific tendency to be more certain than the evidence justifies, and it affects careful, intelligent people and experts too."},{"think":"once you can explain why you made a mistake, you have fixed the bias.","actually":"biases often continue after you know about them. Fixing them takes deliberate changes, such as tracking real times or seeking out evidence against your view."}],"D":[{"think":"random assignment makes findings apply to other populations.","actually":"random assignment makes groups comparable within a study. Applying results more widely depends on random selection."},{"think":"a strong correlation proves causation.","actually":"no correlation, however strong, shows cause and effect. That needs an experiment with random assignment."},{"think":"a double-blind study is a study with two control groups.","actually":"double-blind is about who knows the group assignments: neither participants nor the researchers working with them."},{"think":"a meta-analysis is a better kind of experiment.","actually":"a meta-analysis is not an experiment. It combines existing studies and is only as strong as they are."},{"think":"a case study generalizes well because it is so detailed.","actually":"detail is its strength, but findings about one person may reflect that person's unique situation."}],"E":[{"think":"a negative correlation means there is no relationship.","actually":"a negative correlation is a real relationship in which one variable goes up as the other goes down. Only values near 0 mean little or no relationship."},{"think":"a very strong correlation, such as r = 0.90, proves that one variable causes the other.","actually":"correlation never shows causation, however strong. That needs an experiment with random assignment."},{"think":"percentile rank means the percentage of questions answered correctly.","actually":"percentile rank is the percentage of test-takers who scored at or below you. It says nothing about how many questions you got right."},{"think":"when a top performer's next result drops, pressure or complacency must be the cause.","actually":"it may be regression toward the mean: an extreme score is usually followed by one closer to the person's average."},{"think":"positive skew means most scores are high, with a few high outliers.","actually":"positive skew has its tail on the right: most scores are low, with a few unusually high ones pulling the mean up."}],"F":[{"think":"informed consent and informed assent are the same thing.","actually":"consent is given by an adult participant, or by a parent for a minor. Assent is the minor's own agreement. For participants under 18, both are needed."},{"think":"debriefing happens before the study to prepare participants.","actually":"debriefing always happens after participation: the true purpose and any deception are explained and participants may withdraw their data."},{"think":"deception in research is always unethical.","actually":"it is allowed when the question needs it, the harm is minimal, and full debriefing follows."},{"think":"removing a participant's name guarantees confidentiality.","actually":"a mix of age, job, location, and unusual details can still identify someone without a name."},{"think":"the IRB checks a study's results after it is finished.","actually":"IRB review happens before data collection begins, when the research plan is evaluated."}]}};
